  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🧙 Wizard Name Generator",
      "tagline": "A name with a reputation attached: Aldous the Grey, Hester of Greymere, Archmage Ottilie."
    },
    "intro": "A wizard's name usually comes in two parts: an old-fashioned first name, and something people call them behind their back. This **wizard name generator** gives you both: a grand first name for a wizard of any gender, with an <a href=\"epithet-generator.html\">epithet</a> to suit their school of magic, a place they're known for, a surname, or a title.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Where wizard names come from",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>The word</strong> <em>wizard</em> started out meaning simply a wise man: it's Middle English <em>wys</em>, wise, with the ending <em>-ard</em>.</li><li><strong>Merlin</strong> began as the Welsh <em>Myrddin</em>. Geoffrey of Monmouth turned him into <em>Merlinus</em> in the 1130s, and the Latin name stuck.</li><li><strong>Gandalf</strong> comes from the same Norse list of <a href=\"dwarf-name-generator.html\">dwarves</a> as Thorin and Bombur. <em>Gandálfr</em> probably means \"wand-elf\", which is why Tolkien gave it to his wizard instead.</li><li><strong>Real magicians</strong> liked grand Latin names too. The Swiss physician known as Paracelsus is usually said to have taken a name meaning \"beyond Celsus\", the famous <a href=\"roman-name-generator.html\">Roman</a> writer on medicine.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Four ways to name a wizard",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Name and epithet</strong>: the Tolkien way. Gandalf the Grey, Saruman the White, Radagast the Brown: one word that says what they are.</li><li><strong>Name of a place</strong>: the Forgotten Realms way. Elminster of Shadowdale is known by where he lives. Choose a tower, a fen, a city.</li><li><strong>First name and surname</strong>: the Hogwarts way. Albus Dumbledore is a person with a family, not a legend. Good for a wizard who teaches, or one who's still at school.</li><li><strong>With a title</strong>: Archmage, Magister, Loremaster. For a wizard with an office, a council seat or an opinion of themselves.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Epithets by school of magic",
        "content": "<p>D&amp;D sorts magic into eight schools, and the epithet suits the one you choose:</p><ul><li><strong>Abjuration</strong>, protective magic: <em>the Warded</em>, <em>the Unbroken</em>.</li><li><strong>Conjuration</strong>, summoning: <em>the Many-Handed</em>, <em>the Door-Maker</em>.</li><li><strong>Divination</strong>, seeing what's hidden: <em>the Far-Seeing</em>, <em>the Blind</em> (the blind seer is a very old idea).</li><li><strong>Enchantment</strong>, charm and command: <em>the Silver-Tongued</em>, <em>the Smiling</em>.</li><li><strong>Evocation</strong>, fire, lightning and force: <em>the Red</em>, <em>the Storm-Handed</em>.</li><li><strong>Illusion</strong>: <em>the Many-Faced</em>, <em>the Mirror-Eyed</em>.</li><li><strong>Necromancy</strong>: <em>the Pale</em>, <em>the Deathless</em>.</li><li><strong>Transmutation</strong>, changing one thing into another: <em>the Quicksilver</em>, <em>the Stone-Turner</em>.</li></ul><p>Leave it on Any for the classic colours and characters: the Grey, the Wanderer, the Unready.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "A name with a story",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>True names.</strong> In Ursula Le Guin's Earthsea, knowing something's true name gives you power over it, so wizards hide their own: Ged goes by Sparrowhawk. A wizard with a use-name and a secret name is a plot in two words.</li><li><strong>Name the spells.</strong> D&amp;D's Bigby's Hand and Mordenkainen's Sword are named after characters from Gary Gygax's own games. If your wizard invents a spell, it should carry their name.</li><li><strong>Spell it badly.</strong> Terry Pratchett's Rincewind has WIZZARD stitched on his hat, which tells you everything about him. For more of that, try the <a href=\"funny-wizard-name-generator.html\">Funny Wizard Name Generator</a>.</li></ul><p>Rest of the party need names too? There are generators for every D&D class: <a href=\"barbarian-name-generator.html\">barbarian</a>, <a href=\"bard-name-generator.html\">bard</a>, <a href=\"cleric-name-generator.html\">cleric</a>, <a href=\"druid-name-generator.html\">druid</a>, <a href=\"monk-name-generator.html\">monk</a>, <a href=\"paladin-name-generator.html\">paladin</a>, <a href=\"ranger-name-generator.html\">ranger</a>, <a href=\"rogue-name-generator.html\">rogue</a>, <a href=\"sorcerer-name-generator.html\">sorcerer</a>, <a href=\"warlock-name-generator.html\">warlock</a>. A wizard who specialises in death magic might want the <a href=\"necromancer-name-generator.html\">Necromancer Name Generator</a>.</p><p>Every wizard needs a staff: try the <a href=\"staff-name-generator.html\">Staff Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What's the difference between a wizard, a sorcerer and a warlock?",
        "answer": "In D&D, a wizard learns magic from books and study, a sorcerer is born with it, and a warlock gets it from a pact with a powerful being. Outside D&D the words are often used for the same thing."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are there female wizard names?",
        "answer": "Yes. Choose Female for names like Hester, Ottilie and Seraphina. They work with every format, so you can have Hester the Grey or Archmage Ottilie of the Ninth Tower."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from Tolkien or D&D?",
        "answer": "No. The first names are old real names, and famous wizards like Gandalf, Elminster and Mordenkainen aren't in the lists. For wizards in Tolkien's own style, try the <a href=\"lotr-wizard-name-generator.html\">LOTR Wizard Name Generator</a>."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names in D&D or my book?",
        "answer": "Yes, they're free to use and you don't need to credit the site."
      }
    ]
  }
